Lab 5: Cloud Image Data Anomaly Detection
Objective
Simulate a cloud-based image anomaly detection system by storing sample image data using MinIO (a self-hosted S3-compatible storage system), then applying image similarity techniques with OpenCV to detect anomalies — all locally without internet or AWS/GCP.

Prerequisites
Start your Al Nafi-provided Ubuntu machine

Ensure the following tools are installed:

pip install opencv-python numpy
sudo apt install wget unzip
Download and install MinIO:

wget https://dl.min.io/server/minio/release/linux-amd64/minio
chmod +x minio
sudo mv minio /usr/local/bin/
Steps
Step 1: Set Up a Local Cloud Storage System (MinIO)
Create a data directory and run MinIO server:

mkdir -p ~/minio-data
export MINIO_ROOT_USER=minioadmin
export MINIO_ROOT_PASSWORD=minioadmin
minio server ~/minio-data --console-address ":9001"
Open your browser and access MinIO:

Console: http://localhost:9001

Login with:

Username: minioadmin

Password: minioadmin

Create a bucket (e.g., imagedata) and upload a few sample .jpg or .png images, including one or two unusual ones (anomalies).

Step 2: Download Images from MinIO for Processing
Install MinIO client:

wget https://dl.min.io/client/mc/release/linux-amd64/mc
chmod +x mc
sudo mv mc /usr/local/bin/
Configure client and download images:

mc alias set localminio http://localhost:9000 minioadmin minioadmin
mc ls localminio/imagedata
mc cp --recursive localminio/imagedata ~/minio-images
Step 3: Perform Image Similarity-Based Anomaly Detection
Create a Python script detect_anomalies.py:

import cv2
import os
import numpy as np

def compute_histogram(image_path):
    image = cv2.imread(image_path)
    image = cv2.resize(image, (128, 128))
    hsv = cv2.cvtColor(image, cv2.COLOR_BGR2HSV)
    hist = cv2.calcHist([hsv], [0, 1], None, [50, 60], [0, 180, 0, 256])
    hist = cv2.normalize(hist, hist).flatten()
    return hist

image_dir = "minio-images"
image_files = [os.path.join(image_dir, f) for f in os.listdir(image_dir) if f.endswith(('.jpg', '.png'))]

base_hist = compute_histogram(image_files[0])  # Use first image as baseline
print("Baseline image:", image_files[0])

for img_path in image_files[1:]:
    hist = compute_histogram(img_path)
    score = cv2.compareHist(base_hist, hist, cv2.HISTCMP_CORREL)
    print(f"{img_path} - Similarity: {score:.3f}")
    if score < 0.7:
        print(">> Potential Anomaly Detected!")
Run the script:

python3 detect_anomalies.py
Conclusion
In this lab, you:

Set up MinIO to simulate cloud storage for images

Uploaded and accessed image datasets as if working on AWS S3

Applied OpenCV histogram comparison to detect anomalies in images based on similarity scores

This local setup mimics real-world cloud anomaly detection pipelines and reinforces cloud storage integration with computer vision — fully offline on your Ubuntu system.
